1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,560
CompletableFuture composed asynchronous
stages. Another family of problems looks
different: take a large CPU-bound job,
split it into pieces, solve the pieces,
combine the results.

2
00:00:11,679 --> 00:00:25,119
Sorting, compressing, scanning huge arrays
— divide and conquer. You could submit
every piece to a fixed thread pool by
hand. You would also reinvent work
stealing poorly. What machinery is built
for recursive splitting?

3
00:00:25,299 --> 00:00:35,179
ForkJoinPool is divide-and-conquer with
work stealing. Idle workers steal tasks
from busy workers' queues so cores stay
busy even when splits are uneven.

4
00:00:35,299 --> 00:00:43,859
Parallel streams use this machinery under
the hood — which is why blocking the
common pool hurt in the last episode too.
Look at this java for a moment.

5
00:00:43,979 --> 00:00:50,339
You define a task — typically a
RecursiveTask that returns a value, or a
RecursiveAction that does not.

6
00:00:50,459 --> 00:01:02,899
The task decides whether the range is
small enough to solve directly, or should
fork into subtasks and join their results.
invoke runs the root task and waits for
the answer. Look at this java for a
moment.

7
00:01:03,019 --> 00:01:17,859
Small ranges add directly. Large ranges
split, fork one side, compute the other,
join, and add. The threshold matters: too
large and you under-parallelize; too small
and task overhead dominates.

8
00:01:17,979 --> 00:01:32,379
Unbalanced splits — always peeling one
element — defeat the model. Aim for pieces
that are meaningful work units. Work
stealing in interview language: idle
workers steal tasks from busy workers'
queues.

9
00:01:32,499 --> 00:01:44,019
That sentence explains why the pool
tolerates uneven task sizes better than a
naive global queue of giant chunks. The
common pool is shared across the JVM. Best
for CPU-bound splitting.

10
00:01:44,139 --> 00:01:57,699
Do not block worker threads on I/O or
locks held for long. A blocked worker
cannot steal or run other tasks; under
enough blocking, the pool's parallelism
collapses. Submit blocking work to a
separate executor.

11
00:01:57,819 --> 00:02:11,419
Keep Fork/Join for computation. What if we
treat Fork/Join as a general application
executor for mixed I/O? You will contend
with every parallel stream and many async
defaults for the same workers, then block
them.

12
00:02:11,539 --> 00:02:20,219
That is how "parallelism" becomes
mysterious latency. Parallel streams made
Fork/Join popular even among developers
who never wrote a RecursiveTask.

13
00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:33,620
A library that blocks inside
parallelStream forbids other features from
using the same common pool effectively.
Prefer sequential streams for I/O-bound
work; reserve parallel for measured CPU
gains on large enough data.

14
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:42,900
Fork/Join task sizing is an empirical
craft. Start with a threshold that does
meaningful work — thousands of elements,
not one — and measure.

15
00:02:43,020 --> 00:02:55,300
The steal scheduler is clever; it is not a
substitute for sane task granularity. A
final caution: invoke on a huge task from
a request thread still occupies that
request thread until completion.

16
00:02:55,420 --> 00:03:03,900
Fork/Join parallelizes the work; it does
not automatically make the caller
asynchronous. Divide-and-conquer shines
when subproblems are independent.

17
00:03:04,020 --> 00:03:15,740
Shared mutable accumulators without
atomics or reduction discipline
reintroduce races the pool cannot fix.
Picture summing sensor readings across a
multi-million-element array on a batch
box.

18
00:03:15,860 --> 00:03:27,820
A recursive task splits until chunks fit
in cache-friendly sizes, then adds. Wall-
clock time drops until you hit cores or
memory bandwidth. Blocking on a database
inside those tasks would waste the win.

19
00:03:27,940 --> 00:03:35,380
Hold the checklist: CPU-bound splits; sane
thresholds; no blocking on workers;
measure before celebrating.

20
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:50,260
Sometimes the data you need is not shared
through a queue or map — it is ambient
per-thread context: a request id, a
principal, a legacy formatter that is not
thread-safe. That temptation has a name,
and a leak story.
